the force released two years ago I had  high hopes for the division I love the  thought of an open world Manhattan I'm a  sucker for post-apocalyptic stories and  I'm also a longtime fan of Tom Clancy  games and their tactical combat so like  some I was disappointed when I finally  played it instead of a seamless open  world to explore with my friends I found  the skeleton of a game built around an  artificial loot grind what's more that  Luc Ryan wasn't all that rewarding or  even fun to begin with I was completing  fetch quests and shooting bullet sponge  enemies over and over again lastly I  felt as if developer massive  entertainment had failed to use its  premise to good effect playing as  peacekeeping agents sent into a post  outbreak New York seemed like a  springboard for grim storytelling but  here too the game fell flat characters  were cheesy and any interesting  questions massive raised were brushed  aside in favor of loot and boring  reasons to find it suffice it to say I  bounced off the division hard but of  course the game was a commercial  juggernaut it was the most successful  new franchise to date selling 330  million dollars in its first five days  eclipsing Bungie's destiny by 5 million  in the same period whatever problems I  had with the game didn't seem to  register with a huge number of people  and those numbers have only grown just  two weeks ago Ubisoft announced that the  division had accrued 20 million total  players much like Ubisoft Studios have  done with Rainbow six siege and Ghost  Recon wildlands they've constantly  supported the division in its post  release months its popularity has  allowed the team to implement continual  updates and those updates have kept  increasing the game's popularity it's a  symbiotic relationship that has more for  the division into something very  different than its original form and of  course just recently massive announced  it will be showing the division 2 at e3  this year a sequel was inevitable for  the most successful new franchise of all  time but still it piqued my curiosity  once again I decided to finally give the  division a second chance to see how it  had changed see whether I could embrace  it for what it is now what I want it to  be so this is my experience with the  division 2 years and 20 million players  later  [Music]  let's get the biggest roadblocks out of  the way up front the division is still a  grind it is not a tactical shooter it's  open world is not teeming with memorable  characters and revelatory secrets it is  still largely focused on pushing you  through a gameplay loop of traveling  shooting looting and crafting it is  still Ubisoft main attempt to rival  lifestyle games such as destiny but only  two hours into my recent playthrough it  was apparent just how much more variety  there is this time around in your search  for loot you can hunt down bounties  clear contaminated areas extract virus  samples and defend allied checkpoints  that's not to mention endgame content  such as high-value targets the wave  based resistance mode the 4v4 skirmish  arena the randomized underground  missions or server wide global events  some of these were paid DLC while year  two expansions were all free updates the  division is still very much a grind but  in general that grind is now much more  fun the process is also much more  rewarding enemies constantly drop items  useful for your character rank as I work  to increase my endgame ranking I was  getting new rewards for everything I did  of course that doesn't mean you can't  get attached to your favorite loop in  update 1.3 the division added the  recalibration station allowing you to  re-roll item stats to better suit your  character this seemingly minor addition  is actually huge it lets you keep gear  with high armor rating without  sacrificing the build you're working  toward I can have my cake and eat it too  all-in-all the division does a much  better job of letting me play how I want  there are certain activities that will  undoubtedly provide better loot drops  but in the long haul any time I spend  with a division will likely improve my  character there are more ways to get  loot there are more ways to customize my  play style and there are more ways to  explore this crumbling man hat  [Music]  when it comes to the division's game  world I'm torn I agree with many that  there is wasted potential here a  diseased New York perpetually covered in  holiday decorations with warring  factions trying to survive it's a dream  premise for many writers sadly that  story never really flourishes its  factions for instance have some of the  weakest motivations I can imagine the  city sanitation workers all came  together as the flamethrower toting  cleaners they walked the city burning  everything in sight people included  hoping to rid the world of the new  plague the Rikers on the other hand are  all escaped convicts from the infamous  Rikers Island prison they forms beneath  a murderous leader hoping to use their  criminal ways to claim New York for  their own if you're thinking this all  sounds ham-fisted I agree  furthermore the game had the opportunity  to ask questions about whether you and  your fellow division agents are actually  the good guys you're sent in to maintain  the government during the collapse and  you're given free reign to kill anyone  threatening that agenda but anytime the  division approaches tougher subject  matter it stumbles opting instead to  justify our actions in service to the  greater loot right  and I've got a hell of a lot more than  good intentions we're gonna do this and  we're gonna do it the right way all of  this being said though I am thoroughly  impressed by the world design itself  it's not on par with Los Santos or  breadth of the wilds Hyrule but as far  as settings go massives Manhattan is  beautiful in its modern desolation it's  also remarkable how well the game  manages to fit a third-person cover  based shooter so fluidly into this world  each Street is a potential ambush point  every alleyway a good snipers  line-of-sight massives New York  comprises a multitude of smaller shooter  arenas sewn together into one seamless  hole it's hard to ignore the craft on  display here and this all brings me to  the dark zone what was probably the  game's main attraction before release  soon became a source of headaches for  players and developers alike the center  of Manhattan was the only original PvP  arena in the division allowing agents to  farm high-end loot while also teaming up  with or betraying other human players  doing the latter meant going rogue  essentially painting a target on your  back and making you a lucrative bounty  for deer seeking players um  major problem with all this was balance  or lack thereof at first the penalties  for dying as a rogue agent were so steep  that almost no one ever risked it as new  updates rolled out those consequences  softened however each new update brought  a new wave of cheaters who exploited the  game for seemingly impossible loot drops  by entering the dark zone with extremely  unbalanced gear these players pretty  much ran the PvP arena without any real  opposition toss in the fact that all you  had to do to go rogue was to shoot  another player and the dark zone became  a chaotic nightmare players were jumping  into my line-of-sight forcing me to  become an outlaw against my will  shortly before killing me and claiming  their bounty thankfully the recent  update 1.8 fixed that problem to go  rogue now you have to notify other  players before attacking them this may  sound sort of goofy and gamified but it  works well the tension and mind games  that accompany loot extractions are  still tantalizing as ever not knowing  whether someone will help you fend off  AI enemies or turn on you at the last  second is downright terrifying it's an  intriguing look at your trustworthiness  and your ability to trust in this  chaotic environment full as it is with a  legendary gear ripe for the taking there  are still balance issues in the dark  zone as many players will inevitably  have better weapons than others but the  changes massive have made give me hope  for the dark zone and the sequel in fact  the entirety of the division feels like  an ambitious experiment finally yielding  results the more time massive and  Ubisoft have spent with it the more they  seem to understand what makes it tick  and the more they can shape it into  something unique it's one of those  series that feels on the cusp of  excellence if only it could take those  last few necessary steps  I just hope massive avoids the missteps  Bungie recently made in making a sequel  that didn't seem to learn all that much  from its predecessor for several months  after destiny 2 is released it felt as  if Bungie ignored the progress they had  made with the first game's patches  updates and expansions I don't mean to  keep kicking destiny while it's down it  just feels as if Bungie takes one step  forward for every two steps back and  while we're on the topic of similar  games it's no secret that the division  takes cues from Diablo 3 both our loot  grinds both have item recalibration  procedural late-game dungeons and gear  sets that lend more  personality to your equipment in general  both games are about finding loot  optimizing it and using it to get better  loot until you have the best items  possible for X amount of time but I  think the division is chasing Diablo 3  in a wider sense - when it first  released Diablos grind was far less  rewarding than it is now players spent  much more time seeking the specific gear  they wanted and unique items with  noticeable abilities were few and far  between as the dungeon crawler evolved  however the rewards became more constant  legendary items had more personality  loot was more useful and the time you  spent was more likely to be worth it  judging from the time I put into the  divisions late-game content it still has  problems to fix before it reaches its  full potential exotic items should stand  out farther from the crowd and they  should help me more in my pursuit of  specific character builds but like  Diablo massive has shown it can make the  whole grind more rewarding it just needs  to capitalize in the sequel the  developers have already said they're  making the division - with all of their  recent learning in mind they've said  they're using the new and improved  division 1 as a starting point for  future success I hope this rings true  I hope they pull it off the division is  much better than I remember it being in  2016 and if massive truly learned from  this entire process it might not take me  two years to give it sequel the  attention it deserves
 

